Z||000009||Greg Piet||01-12-2001||06:37 PM||||yes Eibach makes 3 types of springs.<BR>the Pro kit which is general performance variabkle spring rate(which is the only one ive seen for the Fiero)<BR>the roadrace kit which slams the car, and could just as well be aluminum cylinders instead of shocks, and the last one is the <BR>sportline kit, which appears to be a BIG drop of the car(ricey????)<P>Dont know if this is an accurate description, but my friends 85 RX7 just got susp techniques, and I just did Eibachs...<BR>MINE IS MUCH STIFFER, and more responsive.<BR>At mazdatrix they advertise that the spring coefficent of Susp techniques is less than Eibach...<BR>susp tech about 140 while eibach about 190<BR>as far as the eibachs performance alterations as far as lateral g's from stock, ill let you know monday.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>SCCA Region 72<BR>#47 CSP<BR>Bring it to the track.<BR>Lets see if all those mods really helped!||||reg||1
Z||000010||2birds||01-12-2001||09:42 PM||||I have ST's with poly, and I think if I could do it over I'd spend a little extra get the Eibach's.  I'd give up the extra 1/2" drop for a little better ride, and maybe my front tires (215/60/15) wouldn't rub at full lock.  The Eibach's are variable rate, and the ST's are linear.  Between the poly and the springs, my tires do more bump absorption than the springs.<P>You can see how low my red 86 GT is at <A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A>  members section<P>Anybody want to trade their Eibach's for my ST's?  ||||reg||1
